        {
          "tool_use_id": "toolu_01FwMKQzfCL1o7jNm9TLfKrE",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022pages/concepts/log-metric-correlation.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Log-Metric Correlation\\ndescription: Propagating trace identifiers into logs and metric exemplars to enable pivoting between observability signals.\\ntimestamp: 2026-07-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Observability\\n  - concept/OpenTelemetry\\n  - concept/Correlation\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022Single authoritative source describing the correlation mechanism; implementation details not independently verified.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\nLog-metric correlation is the practice of propagating trace and span identifiers into log records and metric exemplars, enabling operators to move fluidly between observability signals.\\n\\n## Mechanism\\n\\nWhen a span is active during logging or metric recording, its \\u0060trace_id\\u0060 and \\u0060span_id\\u0060 are automatically captured and attached as metadata. This creates a forward and backward navigation path:\\n\\n- **Metric spike to traces:** An operator observing an anomalous metric can inspect its exemplars (specific instances tagged with trace identifiers), then jump to the full trace context to understand causality and sequencing.\\n- **Trace to logs:** Within a distributed trace, the operator can pivot to all log lines that occurred during the same span, providing textual context for the measured behavior.\\n\\nThis approach works because the same identifiers unify all three signal types (traces, metrics, logs) into a coherent causal narrative, rather than requiring manual correlation across separate systems.\\n\\n## Use case\\n\\nLog-metric correlation is essential in microservice and cloud-native environments where a single user request traverses many services and produces hundreds of events across multiple backends. Without correlation, an operator must reconstruct causality by guessing time windows and service boundaries. With it, the trace ID acts as a natural, automatic join key.\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n[^1]: OpenTelemetry specification on trace context propagation and exemplars.\\n\u0022}"
        }
      ],
